<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Hamidids</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1391 CE (approx.) · capital Eğirdir</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkish beylik of the Lakes Region of south-western Anatolia, ruled from Egirdir, whose sale of lands helped the Ottomans expand.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felek-al-din-dundar-bey">Felek al-Din Dundar Bey</a>, grandson of Hamid Bey, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, established himself around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egirdir">Egirdir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isparta">Isparta</a> at the beginning of the 14th century. A branch of the family took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a> and became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/teke">Teke</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Hamidids ruled the lake district around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egirdir">Egirdir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isparta">Isparta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burdur">Burdur</a> and Uluborlu, a land of orchards and pastures on the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> and the coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felek-al-din-dundar-bey">Dundar Bey</a> was executed in 1324 by the Ilkhanid governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurtash-chupanid">Timurtash</a>, but the family returned after he fell. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> built mosques and turned a Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserai</a> in Egirdir into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1374 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kemal-al-din-husayn-bey">Kemal al-Din Husayn Bey</a> sold several towns, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beysehir">Beysehir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksehir">Aksehir</a>, to the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> annexed the rest in 1391.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hamidids.jpg" alt="Hamidids" width="320" height="162"><figcaption>Image: Lord Leatherface at Turkish Wikipedia — English translation by Morningstar1814 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beylicats_d%25u2019Anatolie_vers_1330-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1391 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Eğirdir</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Felek al-Din Dundar Bey, Kemal al-Din Husayn Bey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hamidid Beylik, Hamidoğulları</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1346&amp;focus=hamidids" aria-label="Hamidids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5438 809 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hamidids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5758 1065L5709 970M5758 1065L5770 1104"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5770 1104h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5758 1065h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5758 1065h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1346&amp;focus=hamidids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
